<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  An `NDATA` clause is a containment the document STATES, not one it NESTS.

  M60 containment ledger, residual finding. `<!ENTITY logo SYSTEM "l.png" NDATA png>`
  names a notation the DTD declares elsewhere, and necessarily EARLIER: XML 1.0 §4.5
  writes "NDATA NotationName" and §4.2 defines that name as identifying the notation of the
  entity. So the notation's declaration is never inside the entity's, and the edge

    test   -contains-> JPEG
    graphic -contains-> GIF

  is a true containment in the reading "this entity declaration mentions this notation"
  and an impossible one in the reading "this notation is nested inside this entity".

  Measured over the full 11,464-file admitted population: exactly 2 edges, in
  libxslt/tests/general/bug-21-.xml and libxslt/tests/general/bug-52.xml, and in both the
  target's span lies entirely before the source's. The edge carried
  `evidenceClass: "structural"`, which is a claim that NESTING was observed. None was.

  The tier is the fix and not the label, because `usage_contextual` already defines this
  exact shape — "a containment the document states through an attribute rather than through
  nesting" — and its listed precedents are the DTD content model and the NOTATION attribute
  type. Relabelling the two edges `references` would be worse: `references` resolution has
  four classes and an NDATA clause is not one of them.

  The shape below writes the notation declaration AFTER the entity, which no real DTD would
  do — a real one must declare it first — and the edge's target is outside its source either
  way. Real corpus files cannot be quoted in a fixture because they are the corpus's, so
  this one carries the general shape and the EDGE assertions are what pin the behaviour: the
  edge is emitted, its role is `dtd_ndata_notation`, and its tier is `usage_contextual`.
-->
<!DOCTYPE photo [
  <!ENTITY logo SYSTEM "l.png" NDATA png>
  <!ELEMENT photo (caption)>
  <!ATTLIST photo xmlns CDATA #IMPLIED>
  <!NOTATION png SYSTEM "png.exe">
]>
<photo xmlns="&logo;">&caption;</photo>
